+// ---- pipeline compiles off the caller's thread --------------------------------------------------------------------------
+// PanVK compiles a pipeline's shaders on the calling thread: one of iOS 27's compute shaders is 24 522 NIR instructions and
+// took 25 s of a Mali-G72's CPU, and a Preferences fragment shader never ended. On backboardd's or an app's main thread that
+// is the UI frozen for all of it. So a compile runs on a worker (at most three at once, QOS utility); the synchronous
+// creation calls wait for it at most STARBITE_M2V_COMPILE_TIMEOUT_MS (default 5000, 0 = wait for ever) and then fail like any
+// refused pipeline (the caller goes without the effect, or asks again); the compile goes on in the background, its result
+// waits for the next request of the same pipeline (key: the functions' bit-code hashes and the state) and the runtime's
+// caches keep it. The completion-handler creation calls were synchronous too (the handler ran after the compile, on the
+// caller's thread): they now return at once and compile on the worker with no limit.
